<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pre Rup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 961</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Prê Rub</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pre Rup is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, built as the state temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king Rajendravarman and dedicated in 961 or early 962. A temple mountain of brick, laterite and sandstone, it rises in three steep tiers to a summit platform with five towers set in a quincunx, and stands just south of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baray">Baray</a>, aligned with the East Mebon of the same reign. It was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and probably occupies the site of an earlier Shaivite ashram built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasovarman-i">Yasovarman I</a>. Its modern name, meaning 'turn the body', reflects a common belief among Cambodians that funerals were held there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pre-rup.jpg" alt="Pre Rup" width="330" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pre_Rup,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-16,_DD_13.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple stands on a square plan with two perimeter walls. The outer enclosure is a platform bounded by a laterite wall measuring 117 metres from north to south and 127 metres from east to west, entered from the east by a laterite causeway, which a modern road now cuts across. Its four outer gopuras are cross-shaped, with a central brick section and a sandstone vestibule on either side. The laterite and brick give the building a reddish tone that deepens in early morning and late afternoon light.</p><p>Inside the eastern gate stand two groups of three towers aligned north to south, probably later additions by Jayavarman V; one of them appears never to have been built or to have been taken down. Through a second gate, libraries flank the walkway on the second platform, and long separate galleries run along each side, a feature of 10th-century architecture that gave way to a continuous gallery from Ta Keo onward. A stone basin before the entrance, sometimes called a cistern, is thought by scholars to have been the base of a bronze statue of Nandi rather than a place for cremation rites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The pyramid itself measures 50 metres at its base and rises a dozen metres to a summit platform 35 metres square. Twelve small shrines surround its lowest tier. Carved deities guard either side of the eastern door of the central tower, whose other doors are false. The four corner towers once held statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> in the south-west, Uma in the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> in the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in the north-east. An inscription on the doorjambs of the north-east tower dates from Jayavarman VI and is the only proof of his reign at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The scholar Philippe Stern suggested that Pre Rup stood at the centre of a new capital built by Rajendravarman, bounded to the north by the southern dike of the East Baray. No dwellings of such a city survive, and this eastern city hypothesis has never been confirmed by archaeological finds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pre Rup” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rite by which Jayavarman II made himself god-king in 802 was taken from the Hindu tradition, and the royal cult was housed in temples to Shiva and Vishnu, from <b>Pre Rup</b> to Angkor Wat.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
